`SelectCoinsBnB` marks its result as completed when it exhausts the search space before reaching `TOTAL_TRIES`. Only the incomplete case is checked in the unit tests (`bnb_exhaustion_with_solution_test`), and the `bnb_finds_min_waste` fuzz target only reads the flag to decide whether to assert optimality, so reporting false goes unnoticed.

This PR checks `GetAlgoCompleted()` in `TestBnBSuccess` for cases that finish below the attempt limit (100,000).

Kills the following mutant (https://mutanthub.space/mutants/1096):
```diff
--- a/src/wallet/coinselection.cpp
+++ b/src/wallet/coinselection.cpp
@@ -233,7 +233,7 @@ util::Result<SelectionResult> SelectCoinsBnB(std::vector<OutputGroup>& utxo_pool
             if (curr_selection.empty()) {
                 // Exhausted search space before running into attempt limit
                 is_done = true;
-                result.SetAlgoCompleted(true);
+                result.SetAlgoCompleted(false);
                 break;
             }
             // Set `next_utxo` to one after last selected, then deselect last selected UTXO
```

Part of #36692. Please view this PR in context of changes like https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36694.

`CTransaction` gains a constructor that allows direct declarative construction as an alternative to building through `CMutableTransaction`. `CTxIn`'s constructors gain an optional argument to pass the script witness.

It is a conscious decision that `vin/vout` are passed as generic input ranges and that only *their elements* are copied/moved.
Passing the `std::vector` by value and then moving the *container itself* into place would allow the following:

```cpp
std::vector<CTxIn> inputs = ...;
CTxIn& backdoor = inputs.back();

const CTransaction tx{std::move(inputs), ...};

backdoor = ...; // <- modify `tx`, potentially breaking its invariant
```
